## 2. No authorization checks

### Problem

Authentication proves who the user is. Authorization determines what they are allowed to access.

Even if authentication is added, the current design does not distinguish between normal users, admins, service accounts, etc.

### Risk

A logged-in low-privilege user could potentially access all users.

### Recommendation

Add authorization middleware.

Example:

```js
function requireRole(role) {
  return function (req, res, next) {
    if (!req.user || req.user.role !== role) {
      return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Forbidden' });
    }

    return next();
  };
}

app.get('/users', authenticate, requireRole('admin'), (req, res) => {
  res.json([
    { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com' },
  ]);
});
```

If normal users should only access their own profile, use a route like:

```js
app.get('/me', authenticate, (req, res) => {
  res.json({
    id: req.user.sub,
    name: req.user.name,
    email: req.user.email,
  });
});
```

