## 7. No CORS Policy

### Issue

The app does not configure CORS.

By default, browsers cannot freely read responses cross-origin unless CORS headers are set. So the current absence of CORS is not inherently vulnerable.

However, if CORS is added later with a permissive policy like this:

```js
app.use(cors());
```

or:

```js
app.use(cors({ origin: '*' }));
```

then sensitive endpoints could become accessible from arbitrary browser origins.

### Recommendation

If browser-based clients need access, explicitly allow known origins.

```js
const cors = require('cors');

const allowedOrigins = [
  'https://app.example.com',
  'https://admin.example.com',
];

app.use(
  cors({
    origin(origin, callback) {
      if (!origin || allowedOrigins.includes(origin)) {
        return callback(null, true);
      }

      return callback(new Error('Not allowed by CORS'));
    },
    methods: ['GET'],
    allowedHeaders: ['Authorization', 'Content-Type'],
  })
);
```

If credentials/cookies are used:

```js
app.use(
  cors({
    origin: 'https://app.example.com',
    credentials: true,
  })
);
```

Never use `credentials: true` with a wildcard origin.

